WEBVTT


00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:07.070
Welcome to Smart Charging, a hobby project by SCKC. When Intelligent Octopus Go supplies a low-cost

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:12.650
EV charging window, the app schedules your FoxESS home battery to charge too.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.850
This also prevents the battery from supplying the car.

00:00:17.658 --> 00:00:24.918
Use an ESP32-S3 N16R8 with 16 MB of flash and 8 MB of octal memory.

00:00:25.318 --> 00:00:29.138
Prepare a USB data cable and 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi.

00:00:29.598 --> 00:00:32.318
In FoxESS enable Mode Scheduler.

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:34.658
Set Remaining Time Mode to Self-Use.

00:00:36.492 --> 00:00:40.092
Open ESP32 Web Deploy from the project Readme.

00:00:40.532 --> 00:00:42.732
Use desktop Chrome for USB setup.

00:00:43.192 --> 00:00:45.052
Safari cannot perform deployment.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:48.172
Mac and Windows setup apps provide the same flow.

00:00:48.672 --> 00:00:51.292
Close other serial tools before connecting the board.

00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:54.997
Choose New Setup or Reinstall.

00:00:55.577 --> 00:00:58.997
This clean installation erases board settings and saved history.

00:00:59.377 --> 00:01:01.597
Confirm the supported model and deletion.

00:01:01.937 --> 00:01:07.157
Type Erase. Select Erase All and Install, then choose the USB serial port.

00:01:07.697 --> 00:01:11.657
After verification, select Continue and connect ESP32.

00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:13.297
Choose the port again.

00:01:15.117 --> 00:01:17.977
For a configured board, choose Edit Settings instead.

00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:21.397
This reads saved settings without reinstalling firmware.

00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:25.757
If you previously enabled a Config Password, unlock the saved values.

00:01:26.297 --> 00:01:28.377
Otherwise, the tool loads them automatically.

00:01:29.037 --> 00:01:30.797
Sensitive fields stay masked.

00:01:31.177 --> 00:01:33.077
Use an eye button to reveal one field.

00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:41.307
Go to network, scan for Wi-Fi and select your network, enter its password then run test Wi-Fi with

00:01:41.327 --> 00:01:45.407
DHCP this checks the connection and obtains your network addresses.

00:01:45.967 --> 00:01:47.727
A trial does not save the settings.

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:51.047
Wi-Fi and IP verification remain required.

00:01:52.908 --> 00:01:59.548
Keep Dashboard Port 8787, prefer a DHCP reservation on your router for a stable address.

00:02:00.088 --> 00:02:01.828
A fixed IP is also available.

00:02:02.368 --> 00:02:06.328
If you use the suggestion, review the router address pool before accepting it.

00:02:06.768 --> 00:02:08.748
A silent device can still own an address.

00:02:10.658 --> 00:02:15.038
Continue to accounts. Enter your Octopus account number and API key.

00:02:15.458 --> 00:02:19.478
Enter your FoxESS inverter serial number and API token.

00:02:19.998 --> 00:02:22.318
The preparation help explains where to find them.

00:02:22.758 --> 00:02:26.378
Keep real credentials masked, especially when sharing screenshots.

00:02:28.408 --> 00:02:34.828
The optional FoxESS Cloud login enables live WebSocket readings, leave it blank to use REST,

00:02:35.208 --> 00:02:41.568
continue to review and save, saving verifies Wi-Fi and IP but does not wait for service API tests,

00:02:42.108 --> 00:02:44.588
keep USB connected until saving completes.

00:02:46.492 --> 00:02:51.272
Open the local dashboard address shown by setup, including port 8787.

00:02:51.992 --> 00:02:54.852
Your phone and computer must reach the same local network.

00:02:55.332 --> 00:02:58.932
Every dashboard displays data cached by the ESP32.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:03.892
Opening another dashboard does not send New Octopus or FoxESS requests.

00:03:05.908 --> 00:03:08.008
In settings, open Account setup.

00:03:08.568 --> 00:03:10.328
Use save accounts to store corrections.

00:03:10.828 --> 00:03:14.568
Select test all APIs to check the configured services in sequence.

00:03:15.168 --> 00:03:17.608
A red service badge means attention is needed.

00:03:18.028 --> 00:03:19.428
Open it to review the problem.

00:03:19.888 --> 00:03:23.168
Wi-Fi and IP changes still require USB setup.

00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:28.893
First deployment enables automation after network settings are saved.

00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:31.153
Keep other automation workers stopped.

00:03:31.593 --> 00:03:34.593
Follow Smart Dispatch protects octopus charging windows.

00:03:35.233 --> 00:03:40.213
Our example also enables weekly charging from 11.30 at night to 5.30 in the morning.

00:03:40.733 --> 00:03:45.153
Select Review and Apply. Check the official REST confirmation in Overview.

00:03:46.908 --> 00:03:50.268
General settings can open dashboard directly on this browser.

00:03:50.768 --> 00:03:53.788
History uses RAM by default and clears after restart.

00:03:54.368 --> 00:03:58.588
Optional hourly flash saving retains hourly records for up to 60 days.

00:03:59.008 --> 00:04:01.248
Enable it only if you want saved history.

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:04.248
Display updates do not write each reading to flash.

00:04:06.117 --> 00:04:10.917
The ESP32 checks GitHub after startup, then every 24 hours.

00:04:11.537 --> 00:04:13.397
Settings can disable these checks.

00:04:13.877 --> 00:04:16.137
A header badge identifies a newer version.

00:04:16.597 --> 00:04:22.317
Installation is manual. During an update, automation suspends temporarily and returns to its saved

00:04:22.337 --> 00:04:25.477
state after verification. Keep power connected.

00:04:27.325 --> 00:04:30.105
The green power button means automation is running.

00:04:30.605 --> 00:04:33.205
Select it and confirm to pause schedule changes.

00:04:33.585 --> 00:04:38.805
Monitoring continues. Keep the ESP32 powered and connected to Wi-Fi.

00:04:39.285 --> 00:04:41.725
You can close the browser and disconnect the computer.

00:04:42.305 --> 00:04:45.265
This edition provides local access without Cloudflare.

00:04:45.765 --> 00:04:46.865
Your setup is ready.
